    <p>
      Now suppose that $p$ satisfied the hypotheses of the proposition. We must show that $p$ has the right lifting property with respect to the classes of morphisms $(1)$, $(2)$, $(3)$, and $(4)$ of Definition <a href="/tag/DA3E0C" data-tag="DA3E0C">3.1.1.1</a>. For $(1)$, this follows from the assumption that $p$ is an inner fibration. For $(2)$, this follows from $(C)$ and from the assumption that every marked edge is $p$-Cartesian. For $(3)$, we are free to replace $S$ by $(\Delta ^2)^{\sharp }$; then $p$ is a Cartesian fibration over an $\infty $-category $S$ and we may apply Proposition <a href="/tag/E39269" data-tag="E39269">2.4.1.7</a> to deduce that the class of $p$-Cartesian edges is stable under composition. 
    <p>
      Finally, for $(4)$, we may replace $S$ by $K^{\sharp }$; then $S$ is a Kan complex and $p$ is a Cartesian fibration, so the $p$-Cartesian edges of $X$ are precisely the equivalences in $X$. Since $K$ is a Kan complex, any map $K \rightarrow X$ carries the edges of $K$ to equivalences in $X$. 
